# =============================================================================
# ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES REFERENCE
# Monitor Lite — reviewed against config.py 2026-08-11 (all env vars are read
# exclusively in config.py — no other module calls os.getenv, confirmed via grep).
# =============================================================================

# CORE — required to start; shared by both tracks below
# TARGET_NAME          - Display name of the monitored bank/site.
# TARGET_URL           - Public URL used for pulse checks.
# CHECK_INTERVAL_S     - The overall heartbeat: how often pulse, render, and the cheap
#                         authed check ALL run, every single cycle, no matter what
#                         session/login state anything is in. NOT the same clock as
#                         LOGIN_INTERVAL_S (AUTH section) -- that one only throttles real,
#                         full logins, and only fires occasionally. It does set the
#                         GRANULARITY of that throttle though: a login attempt can only
#                         start on one of these boundaries, so LOGIN_INTERVAL_S always
#                         rounds up to a whole number of cycles (worked example in the
#                         AUTH section below).
# BROWSER_TIMEOUT_MS   - Timeout (ms) for any browser-driven step in either track (main's
#                         render check and every step of the auth journey).
# BURST_GAP_S          - Idle seconds between confirmation-burst probes, measured from
#                         when the PREVIOUS probe finished. Replaces BURST_DELAYS_S
#                         (retired 2026-08-30, B38): absolute offsets only held the burst to
#                         a fixed footprint while probes were fast enough to leave idle time,
#                         and once a probe overran its slot the schedule became fiction.
#                         25s was chosen by simulation over 1,200 randomised worlds -- it is
#                         the false-positive dial. At 10s six nuisance events paged, at 15s
#                         three, at 25s none.
# BURST_PROBES         - Confirmation probes after the one that opened the run. The burst
#                         re-probes THE LAYER THAT FAILED (B37), not alternating layers.
# WAKE_GRACE_S         - After the monitor notices it was not running (a wall-clock gap
#                         greater than 2x CHECK_INTERVAL_S), probes are recorded but do not
#                         score for this long. Lifts early on the first passing probe. The
#                         bound matters for the opposite case: waking INTO a real outage
#                         means no probe ever passes, and "suppress until a pass" alone would
#                         leave the monitor blind indefinitely. (B7)
# BURST_JITTER_S       - Random jitter (s) added to each burst gap, so restarted or
#                         parallel monitors do not synchronise their re-probes.
# BURST_WINDOW_S       - RETIRED 2026-08-30 (B38). There is no window: DOWN needs
#                         consecutive failures, not failures inside a clock.
#                         config.py default: 90.
# DASHBOARD_USER       - Dashboard basic-auth username.
# DASHBOARD_PASSWORD   - Dashboard basic-auth password.
# PORT                 - HTTP port for the FastAPI dashboard.
# DB_PATH              - Path to the SQLite database file.
# BROWSER_CHANNEL      - Playwright/patchright browser channel (e.g. chrome). Applies to
#                         main track's render check; auth journey always runs headed
#                         under xvfb per Rule 14's v3.3 exception and ignores this.
# HEADLESS             - Headless mode for main track's render check only (see
#                         BROWSER_CHANNEL — auth journey ignores this).

# PULSE + RENDER (main track — "is the login page reachable")
# REQUIRED_TEXT        - Text that must be present on the public login page for it to
#                         count as "rendering". REQUIRED_TEXT OR (REQUIRED_ROLE +
#                         REQUIRED_NAME) is required by validate_core().
# REQUIRED_ROLE        - ARIA role of the required login-page element (alternative to
#                         REQUIRED_TEXT).
# REQUIRED_NAME        - Accessible name of the required login-page element (alternative
#                         to REQUIRED_TEXT).
# DOWN_CONFIDENCE      - Main track's score threshold. Currently INERT by arithmetic: with
#                         MIN_FAILED_PROBES=4 the weakest possible evidence (4 soft failures
#                         worth 1 each) already totals 4. Retained deliberately -- if the
#                         floor is ever lowered to 3, three soft failures total 3 and this
#                         blocks them again. [2026-08-30 / B37]
# MIN_FAILED_PROBES    - Main track's CONSECUTIVE failed probes on one layer, with no
#                         intervening pass of that layer, to declare DOWN. No time window:
#                         a slow probe delays detection, it cannot prevent it.
# EVIDENCE_STALE_AFTER_S - If a layer has gone unprobed this long, its run of consecutive
#                         failures is discarded before the next failure is counted. This is
#                         what replaces BURST_WINDOW_S: evidence now persists until a pass
#                         clears it, and a pass cannot happen while the monitor is not
#                         looking (restart, host asleep, a hung cycle). Without it the
#                         monitor pages on hours-old evidence.
# RECOVERY_PASSES      - Consecutive passes of the causing layer needed to close an
#                         incident. Entering DOWN takes MIN_FAILED_PROBES probes; leaving it
#                         used to take one. [B39]

# AUTH (sign-in journey + session reuse — "can we actually get past login";
# security-team test site ONLY)
# LOGIN_URL              - Login form URL.
# AUTHED_URL             - The authenticated home route the cheap session-reuse check
#                           hits DIRECTLY -- never derived from LOGIN_URL. Login and
#                           authed-home are independent backends (login/MFA/Cloudflare
#                           vs. plain cookie check), so testing them independently is
#                           real, distinct evidence (Rule 16).
# LOGIN_USER             - Bank login username.
# LOGIN_PASSWORD         - Bank login password.
# AUTHED_REQUIRED_TEXT   - Text that must render on the authed page. AUTHED_REQUIRED_TEXT
#                           OR (AUTHED_REQUIRED_ROLE + AUTHED_REQUIRED_NAME) required by
#                           validate_stage6().
# AUTHED_REQUIRED_ROLE   - ARIA role of the required authed-page element (alternative to
#                           AUTHED_REQUIRED_TEXT).
# AUTHED_REQUIRED_NAME   - Accessible name of the required authed-page element
#                           (alternative to AUTHED_REQUIRED_TEXT).
# ERROR_BANNER_TEXT      - Text that must be ABSENT from the post-login page for a login
#                           to count as successful; its presence = credentials rejected.
# MFA_ENABLED            - Describes the SITE, not a testing toggle: whether this bank
#                           requires a second factor after the password. Set once based
#                           on the target and leave it.
# TOTP_SECRET            - Captured authenticator-app secret (from enrollment's "can't
#                           scan?" option); generates a fresh MFA code each attempt.
#                           Required whenever MFA_ENABLED is true, UNLESS
#                           ALLOW_MFA_UNCONFIGURED (below) is set.
# ALLOW_MFA_UNCONFIGURED - YOUR testing toggle, not a site property; off by default. With
#                           TOTP_SECRET blank and this true, the auth track is still
#                           allowed to START (session-reuse checks work normally every
#                           cycle, no MFA involved), but any recovery login eventually
#                           needed can't complete MFA and cleanly reports mfa_failed ->
#                           CONFIG_ERROR instead of refusing to start or crashing.
#                           Recover by running scripts/run_signin_drill.py --keep-session
#                           (pauses for you to complete MFA by hand). Production requires
#                           a real TOTP_SECRET and this set back to false -- never leave
#                           true unattended.
# CHALLENGE_TIMEOUT_MS   - Timeout (ms) waiting for an MFA/challenge prompt.
# LOGIN_STRESS_MODE      - Reserved for a future sanctioned stress-soak window (Stages
#                           6-7 only; deleted at Stage 8 per Rule 11). Not currently
#                           wired to any behavior -- leave false.
# MASK_TEXT              - Semicolon-separated regex patterns redacted from post-login
#                           screenshots (e.g. \$[\d,]+\.\d{2} for dollar amounts). Blank
#                           = no pattern-based masking, but MASKING_ENABLED=true still
#                           applies journey.py's default masking.
# MASKING_ENABLED        - Master toggle for authed-screenshot masking. Keep true;
#                           authed screenshots are always masked by policy -- only false
#                           for a confirmed no-PII test target. [v3.2] patchright is a
#                           named, scoped exception to Rule 12 for this journey only.
#                           [v3.3] journey always runs headed under xvfb, never honors
#                           HEADLESS.
# SESSION_STATE_PATH     - Path to the saved session/cookie state (storageState incl.
#                           cf_clearance) that every cheap authed check reads from --
#                           this is WHAT gets reused, saved after a successful full
#                           login. Real secret: chmod 600, gitignored.
# SESSION_MAX_AGE_S      - How long the saved session file is trusted before being
#                           treated as stale, purely by its age on disk (not by asking
#                           the bank) -- once older than this, the cheap check is skipped
#                           and a real recovery login is attempted instead. Different
#                           clock from LOGIN_INTERVAL_S (that limits how often a LOGIN
#                           can be attempted; this limits how long a SESSION is trusted).
#                           Hand-picked -- Stage 7's soak should replace it with the real
#                           measured session lifetime.
# LOGIN_INTERVAL_S       - Minimum seconds between two real, full (credential + MFA)
#                           login attempts, measured from when the LAST attempt was
#                           RECORDED (success or failure alike). NOT the same as
#                           CHECK_INTERVAL_S (CORE section, which runs every cycle
#                           regardless). [v3.9] This is now the ONLY login rate limit --
#                           MAX_LOGINS_PER_DAY was removed (see below). Hand-picked --
#                           Stage 7's soak should replace it with a measured value.
#
#     HOW THE TWO CLOCKS FIT TOGETHER -- worked example
#     -------------------------------------------------
#     Every CHECK_INTERVAL_S the auth track asks two questions, in order:
#
#       Q1 (SESSION_MAX_AGE_S): do I have a usable session file -- does it exist, and
#                               is it younger than SESSION_MAX_AGE_S?
#           yes -> reuse it. Cheap ~4s check, zero logins. Q2 is never asked.
#           no  -> ask Q2.
#
#       Q2 (LOGIN_INTERVAL_S):  am I allowed to attempt a real login right now -- has it
#                               been at least LOGIN_INTERVAL_S since the last attempt?
#           yes -> full credential+MFA login. On SUCCESS the session file is rewritten.
#           no  -> do nothing this cycle; record `session_expired` (scores 0, Rule 17).
#
#     Q1 gates Q2, which is why SESSION_MAX_AGE_S -- not LOGIN_INTERVAL_S -- sets the
#     normal login cadence.
#
#     HEALTHY DAY, at the shipped 600 / 120:
#       09:00     login succeeds, session file written
#       09:01..09:09  Q1 yes -> reuse the file, ~4s each. Q2 never asked.
#       09:10     file is 600s old -> Q1 no. Q2: last attempt 09:00, 600s ago >= 120 -> yes
#                 -> login, file rewritten.
#       => one login roughly every 10 min (~139/day). LOGIN_INTERVAL_S never blocks
#          anything on a healthy day -- it answered "yes" every time it was asked.
#
#     BROKEN LOGIN (e.g. a renamed field -> element_missing, which does NOT halt the
#     track the way auth_rejected/bot_challenge do): a FAILED login writes no session
#     file, so Q1 answers "no" every single cycle -- there is no file to age out and get
#     replaced, there is simply never a file. Q2 is then the only brake:
#       C+4     Q1 no -> Q2 yes -> login runs ~15s -> FAILS, no file. Recorded at C+19.
#                                  cooldown opens at C+19+120 = C+139
#       C+64    Q1 no -> Q2 no  (45s elapsed)  -> skip
#       C+124   Q1 no -> Q2 no  (105s elapsed) -> skip   <- misses the window by 15s
#       C+184   Q1 no -> Q2 yes (165s elapsed) -> login -> FAILS ...
#       => one attempt every 180s = 20/hour, indefinitely, until a human fixes it.
#
#     NOTE the rate is NOT 3600/LOGIN_INTERVAL_S. Attempts can only start on a
#     CHECK_INTERVAL_S boundary, and the attempt itself burns time before it is
#     recorded, so:
#         spacing = ceil((LOGIN_INTERVAL_S + login_duration_s) / CHECK_INTERVAL_S)
#                   * CHECK_INTERVAL_S
#     At 120 + ~18s that rounds up past the 120s boundary to 180s. Holds steady for
#     login durations from ~2s to ~40s.
#
#     WHY 120 AND NOT MORE. The two clocks start at different moments: Q1's starts when
#     the session file is WRITTEN, Q2's when the login row is RECORDED, and browser
#     teardown can sit between them. A gap where Q1 says "stale" while Q2 still says
#     "wait" opens whenever LOGIN_INTERVAL_S + that skew >= SESSION_MAX_AGE_S. Observed
#     live 2026-08-26: a 300s teardown stall after a SUCCESSFUL login, with both values
#     at 600, produced 5 minutes of false `session_expired` on a session that was
#     working fine. At 120 the config absorbs up to 480s of skew. Keep
#     LOGIN_INTERVAL_S well under SESSION_MAX_AGE_S; never set them equal.
#
#     TWO STARTUP GUARDS ENFORCE THIS -- config.py exits rather than warning:
#
#       FLOOR: LOGIN_INTERVAL_S must be >= 60. A cooldown shorter than one cycle is
#       always already satisfied by the time the next cycle asks, so it would silently
#       limit nothing.
#
#       HEADROOM [B30, 2026-08-29]: SESSION_MAX_AGE_S - LOGIN_INTERVAL_S must be >= 300.
#       The reserved gap -- not a ratio -- is the thing the dead window is made of, and
#       300s is the worst teardown skew actually measured on a successful login, so this
#       is a floor derived from evidence rather than a round number. It is a floor, not
#       a target: the shipped 600/120 leaves 480s. Note this guard also rejects the pair
#       config.py itself used to default to (1800/1800) before B30 corrected the
#       fallbacks to 600/120 -- running without these two variables set previously handed
#       out the exact configuration this paragraph warns against.
#
# A NOTE ON THIS FILE'S SYNTAX [B33, 2026-08-29]
#     config.py refuses to start if ANY line in your .env cannot be parsed as a setting,
#     rather than letting python-dotenv warn once on stderr and carry on. The risk it
#     closes is not the stray note -- it is the mangled ASSIGNMENT: an unparseable line is
#     skipped silently, so os.getenv() falls through to the hardcoded default and the
#     monitor comes up looking healthy on a value nobody chose. Shapes dotenv rejects: a
#     missing '=' (LOGIN_PASSWORD "x"), an unterminated quote, or any prose line. A stray
#     comment missing its '#' is the common case. The error names LINE NUMBERS ONLY and
#     never the contents, since a mangled assignment's value half may be a live credential.
#
# MAX_LOGINS_PER_DAY     - [v3.9] REMOVED -- do not reintroduce without amending Rule 11.
#                           It was a hard daily cap on login attempts. Two reasons it
#                           went: (1) LOGIN_INTERVAL_S already bounds the daily total by
#                           arithmetic (at 120s that is ~480/day worst case), so the cap
#                           only mattered if set BELOW a bound that already exists; and
#                           (2) hitting it was a dead end until UTC midnight -- the auth
#                           track stopped attempting logins while `session_expired` kept
#                           scoring 0 (Rule 17), so the dashboard read UP with no authed
#                           evidence behind it and nothing alerted. A throttle that
#                           recovers by itself beats a breaker that silently blinds the
#                           monitor for the rest of the day. If a total ceiling is ever
#                           wanted back, make exhausting it raise CONFIG_ERROR (like
#                           bot_challenge) so it is loud, not silent.
# AUTH_DOWN_CONFIDENCE   - Auth track's own score threshold to declare DOWN (main
#                           track's pair is DOWN_CONFIDENCE/MIN_FAILED_PROBES above).
#                           Same rule, applied separately: DOWN requires score >=
#                           AUTH_DOWN_CONFIDENCE AND >= AUTH_MIN_FAILED_PROBES CONSECUTIVE
#                           failed probes, no intervening pass. No time window (B38).
#                           Bursts like the main track (cheap, zero-login re-probes), so
#                           uses the same floor.
# AUTH_MIN_FAILED_PROBES - Auth track's minimum distinct failed probes to declare DOWN
#                           (see AUTH_DOWN_CONFIDENCE).

# ALERTING (email + SMS)
# ALERT_CHANNELS       - Comma-separated list of enabled alert channels (email, sms).
#                         Defaults to "email" in config.py.
# RECIPIENTS_EMAIL     - Email alert To: recipients (comma-separated list, e.g.,
#                         ops@bank.com,security@bank.com).
# RECIPIENTS_CC        - Email alert Cc: recipients (comma-separated list, optional).
#                         Visible to all recipients.
# RECIPIENTS_BCC       - Email alert Bcc: recipients (comma-separated list, optional).
#                         Hidden from other recipients (not included in message headers).
# GMAIL_USER           - Gmail account used to send alerts.
# GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD   - Gmail app password for GMAIL_USER.
# TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID   - Twilio account SID. core (config.py) never reads Twilio vars;
#                         monitor/channels/sms_twilio.py does. Blank = SMS channel not
#                         configured; ALERT_CHANNELS stays email-only until these are
#                         filled in AND "sms" is added to ALERT_CHANNELS.
# TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN    - Twilio auth token.
# TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER   - Twilio sending phone number.
# SMS_TO_NUMBER        - Comma-separated list of SMS recipient numbers.
# TWILIO_CONTENT_SID   - Optional; required on Twilio trial accounts (freeform body
#                         sends are rejected there). Set to an approved Content
#                         Template's SID (HXxxxxxxxx...) with a single {{1}} variable.
# SMS_GATEWAY_ADDRESS  - Comma-separated list of <10-digit number>@<carrier gateway
#                         domain> (e.g. 5551234567@vtext.com Verizon, @txt.att.net AT&T,
#                         @tmomail.net T-Mobile, @vmobl.com Visible) — testing SMS via
#                         email gateway, no Twilio account needed. Uses
#                         GMAIL_USER/GMAIL_APP_PASSWORD above -- no separate creds.
